import json
from pathlib import Path
p=Path(__file__).resolve().parent/'draft-cards.json'
x=json.loads(p.read_text())
def add(cid,body,takeaway,**kw):
 n=len(body.split());assert 30<=n<=50,(cid,n)
 x[cid]=dict(body=body,takeaway=takeaway,**kw)
add('Q48970669','Epistemic injustice concerns wronging someone specifically as a knower. In Fricker’s account, prejudice can reduce a speaker’s credibility, while gaps in shared concepts can obstruct understanding of an experience. These mechanisms connect unfair treatment with the everyday practices of telling and interpreting.', 'Distinguish credibility failures from unequal interpretive resources.',source_id='culture-fricker-introduction',locator='Printed page 1, opening definitions and examples',parent_source_id='culture-fricker-introduction',parent_note='Editorial epistemology placement based on practices of knowing.')
add('local:catalog:testimonial-injustice','In Fricker’s account, testimonial injustice occurs when prejudice makes a listener give someone’s words less credibility than they deserve. Her example is police disbelieving a Black speaker because of race. The wrong concerns recognition as a knower, not merely disagreement about a claim.', 'Identify prejudice within judgments of a speaker’s credibility.',source_id='culture-fricker-introduction',locator='Printed page 1, testimonial-injustice definition and police example',parent_source_id='culture-fricker-introduction',parent_note='Fricker explicitly identifies this form of epistemic injustice.')
add('local:catalog:hermeneutical-injustice','In Fricker’s account, hermeneutical injustice arises when unequal shared interpretive resources leave someone unfairly unable to understand a social experience. Sexual harassment before that concept gained recognition illustrates the problem: the obstacle lies partly in collective understanding, rather than only one listener’s disbelief.', 'Locate an interpretive obstacle within unequal shared resources.',source_id='culture-fricker-introduction',locator='Printed page 1, hermeneutical-injustice definition and harassment example',parent_source_id='culture-fricker-introduction',parent_note='Fricker explicitly identifies this form of epistemic injustice.')
add('local:catalog:contributory-injustice','Contributory injustice, in Dotson’s account, occurs when a dominant group refuses interpretive resources that marginalized communities have already developed. The issue is not simply a missing concept. Knowledge can be available within a community yet excluded from the understanding accepted by others.', 'Distinguish refused knowledge from an undeveloped shared concept.',locator='§4.1 Epistemic Injustice, Dotson and Pohlhaus paragraphs, lines 225–228',parent_note='The entry explicitly classifies contributory injustice as epistemic injustice.',limits=['The card attributes this contested account to Dotson.'])
add('local:catalog:metaphysical-grounding','Metaphysical grounding asks what makes a fact hold in virtue of another, rather than what caused it earlier. Philosophers use picketing as constituting a strike to illustrate the distinction. The disputed notion concerns constitutive explanation or determination, not just a necessary connection.', 'Separate constitutive dependence from causal history.',locator='Introduction, lines 13–18; §1.1 Determination and explanation',parent_note='The entry explicitly locates grounding questions within metaphysics.',limits=['Grounding is presented as a disputed philosophical notion.'])
add('local:catalog:truthmaker-theory','Truthmaker theory asks how true representations depend on reality. A truthmaker must be relevant to what a claim is about, rather than merely entail it. What sits behind a refrigerator, for example, cannot explain why a truth of arithmetic holds.', 'Connect truth to relevant reality rather than mere entailment.',locator='§1.1 Truthmaking as Entailment, relevance discussion; §1.2, lines 66–85',parent_note='The entry explicitly treats truthmaking as a metaphysical project.',limits=['Different truthmaker accounts disagree about the relation and its range.'])
add('local:catalog:truthmaker-maximalism','Truthmaker maximalism claims that every truth requires something in the world to make it true. The strain appears with negative truths: if absolutely nothing existed, what could make that true? This challenges the doctrine unless one rejects the possibility of an empty world.', 'Test a universal truthmaker requirement against negative truths.',locator='§2.1 Maximalism, lines 181–203',parent_note='The entry identifies maximalism as a position within truthmaker theory.',limits=['The challenge assumes that an empty world is possible; it is not a refutation accepted by all theorists.'])
add('local:catalog:fitting-attitude-theories-of-value','Fitting-attitude theories explain value through responses that an object merits. Being admirable, for example, is explained through the fittingness of admiration. The claim concerns appropriate responses, not whatever people happen to feel, so actual affection or dislike alone does not settle an object’s value.', 'Distinguish a deserved response from an actual feeling.',locator='Introduction, lines 13–20',parent_note='The entry explicitly presents these as theories explaining value.')
add('local:catalog:wrong-kind-of-reason-problem','The wrong kind of reason problem challenges certain fitting-attitude theories of value. A demon’s threat might give someone reason to value a worthless cup of mud. The incentive concerns having the attitude, while leaving the object’s value unexplained; theories must distinguish these reasons.', 'Separate reasons to adopt an attitude from an object’s value.',locator='§3.1 The Wrong Kind of Reason Problem, lines 167–175',parent_note='The entry explicitly identifies this challenge to fitting-attitude theories.',limits=['The challenge applies to certain formulations; the entry discusses competing responses.'])
add('local:catalog:khipu','Khipus recorded information in the Inca Empire through cords and knots. Knot positions can represent decimal places, while twists, attachments, and colors provide further distinctions whose meanings scholars investigate. Numerical organization therefore depends on a cord’s structure, rather than an alphabetic sequence of written words.', 'Read decimal organization in knots while retaining interpretive uncertainty.',locator='What is a khipu?; Encoding the Khipu; Khipu Uses, lines 132–157 and 195',parent_note='NIST explicitly identifies khipu use throughout the Inca Empire.')
add('local:catalog:chaski','Chaskis were official messengers in the Inca Empire. Khipus could be wrapped into portable bundles for them to carry across its territory. The example shows how a system of recorded information depended on transport as well as the cords used to encode it.', 'Connect portable records with an imperial messenger network.',locator='Khipu Uses, lines 195–196',parent_note='Editorial imperial-administration placement; NIST identifies official Inca messengers.')
add('local:catalog:lukasa-met-1977-467-3','This Luba memory board communicates through incised designs and relief carving, whereas many other lukasa use attached beads and shells. The Met’s interpretation connects its motifs with political history and territory. Senior Mbudye members learned to decipher the board within a specialized knowledge tradition.', 'Examine how this carved board supports trained historical interpretation.',locator='Curatorial description, lines 10–16; Artwork Details, object number 1977.467.3',gap='Individual artifact retained; a generic Lukasa parent is absent from approved and baseline targets.',limits=['Motif meanings are attributed to the museum’s curatorial interpretation.'])
add('local:catalog:mbudye-association','Mbudye is a Luba association charged with preserving and interpreting political and historical principles. Its councils could check or reinforce chiefly power. Members progressed through levels of knowledge, with those at the highest level authorized to interpret the intricate designs of lukasa memory boards.', 'Connect specialized historical knowledge with checks on chiefly power.',locator='Curatorial description, lines 12–14',parent_note='Editorial placement based on the association’s documented Luba state role.')
add('local:catalog:paiza-met-1993-256','This late-thirteenth-century Yuan safe-conduct pass carries an imperial inscription in Phakpa script. Silver fills an openwork design so its characters project on both sides, reversed on the back. The artifact connects official authority with the material construction and visibility of its written command.', 'Read imperial authority through an inscription’s material construction.',locator='Curatorial description, lines 7–11; Artwork Details, object 1993.256',parent_note='Editorial Yuan-dynasty placement based on the museum’s object attribution.')
add('local:catalog:sanke-mon','Sanké Môn is an annual collective fishing ritual in San, Mali, commemorating the town’s foundation. Songs, dances, and other practices connect the gathering with local memory and belief. Protecting the Sanké pond preserves both an ecological resource and the setting that sustains the ritual.', 'Connect a collective ritual with its environmental setting.',locator='Main French text, lines 24–26 and 34–35',parent_note='UNESCO explicitly identifies Sanké Môn as a collective ritual.')
add('local:catalog:negro-motorist-green-book','Published between 1936 and 1966, with a wartime interruption, the Green Book listed businesses welcoming African American travelers. Reports from readers and postal-worker contacts helped build its directory. Hotels, restaurants, and service stations turned shared local knowledge into a practical response to segregation.', 'Trace how shared information supported travel under segregation.',locator='Publication and directory history, lines 24–35',parent_note='Editorial historical placement based on the documented segregation context.')
add('local:catalog:port-chicago-mutiny-trial','After the 1944 Port Chicago explosion, 258 Black sailors refused renewed ammunition-loading work; fifty were convicted of mutiny. Their case exposed the intersection of dangerous work and racial discrimination in the Navy. The Secretary of the Navy exonerated the sailors on July 17, 2024.', 'Compare an unjust wartime conviction with later exoneration.',locator='The Aftermath and Mutiny; Trial Proceedings; Exoneration, lines 39–47 and 55',parent_note='Editorial placement based on documented segregation in the US Navy.',limits=['Conflicting source statements about explosion causation and testimony are excluded.'])
add('local:catalog:civilian-public-service','During World War II, US Civilian Public Service offered conscientious objectors work outside military command. Government authorization combined with funding and administration by peace churches. At Patapsco, participants maintained parks and fought fires without wages, showing the practical costs of this route for pacifists.', 'Examine unpaid civilian work as a historical pacifist alternative.',locator='Civilian Public Service; In Camp; Challenges, lines 44–59',parent_note='Editorial application of pacifism based on documented conscientious-objector service.')
p.write_text(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print(len(x))
